Q1 : How does the CopyOnWriteArrayList differ from a synchronized ArrayList in Java, and when would you choose one over the other?

CopyOnWriteArrayList and a synchronized ArrayList (achieved using Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList())) are both thread-safe 
list implementations in Java, but they differ significantly in their approach to concurrency and performance characteristics.
Key Differences:
Synchronization Mechanism:
Synchronized ArrayList: Uses intrinsic locks (monitors) to synchronize access to the underlying ArrayList. Every method call on the synchronized list, 
including reads and writes, acquires a lock on the list object, ensuring only one thread can access it at a time.
CopyOnWriteArrayList: Achieves thread safety by creating a new copy of the underlying array whenever a mutative operation (add, set, remove) occurs. 
Read operations, however, operate on the existing, immutable array, requiring no synchronization.

Read vs. Write Performance:
Synchronized ArrayList: Read and write operations are equally impacted by synchronization overhead, as both require acquiring a lock.
CopyOnWriteArrayList: Read operations are generally very fast as they don't involve locking. Write operations, however, 
can be significantly slower due to the overhead of copying the entire array.

Iterator Behavior:
Synchronized ArrayList: Iterators are fail-fast, meaning they throw a ConcurrentModificationException if the underlying 
list is modified structurally by another thread while an iteration is in progress. Iteration must be performed within a synchronized block.
CopyOnWriteArrayList: Iterators are fail-safe and provide a snapshot of the list at the time the iterator was created. 
They will not throw ConcurrentModificationException even if the list is modified concurrently, but they will reflect the 
state of the list at the time of their creation, not subsequent changes.


When to Choose One Over the Other:
Choose CopyOnWriteArrayList when:
Read operations vastly outnumber write operations: The performance benefit of lock-free reads outweighs the cost of copying on writes.
You need fail-safe iterators: When concurrent modifications during iteration are acceptable, and you want to avoid ConcurrentModificationException.
List size is relatively small: The overhead of copying the entire array on writes becomes more manageable.
Choose Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList()) when:
Write operations are frequent, or comparable to read operations: The overhead of copying in CopyOnWriteArrayList would be detrimental.
You need strong consistency during iteration: Fail-fast iterators are preferred to detect concurrent modifications.
List size can be very large: Copying large arrays frequently would be inefficient.
